            ]
          },
          {
            "key": "english_spanish_common",
            "text": "The authors note that both English and Spanish are very common on Twitter, so any country predominantly using these two languages will have a good representation by default.",
            "citations": [
              {
                "paper_id": "2004.00809",
                "chunk_id": "c-01b9a363a68a29a0e9af4ff8"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "key": "wealthy_urban_hypothesis",
            "text": "The authors hypothesize that Twitter data matches populations better when weighted by GDP and worse by internet-usage statistics, implying Twitter represents more wealthy populations; an alternate interpretation is that their Twitter collection is based on urban areas, which tend to have wealthier populations.",
            "citations": [
              {
                "paper_id": "2004.00809",
                "chunk_id": "c-9887004ca215701b79e8e570"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "key": "twitter_false_positives",
            "text": "The authors note that there are more false positives in Twitter data and that more research is needed to distinguish immigration, tourism, and business as alternate sources of false-positive languages.",
